{%- comment -%}
  Is the human-in-the-loop gate enforced for this call? (spec §9.6)

  Two sources, in order:

  1. The app's approval partial (config.approval_partial, default
     'mcp/approval'), when it exists. An app that lets its own admins turn the
     gate on and off keeps that switch in its own settings and answers here, so
     the engine never has to be told to read an app's table. The partial is
     given the tool name, so an app can answer for one tool instead of for all
     of them at once: a desk may let an assistant add an internal note straight
     away while a reply to a customer still waits for a person. A partial that
     does not declare the parameter simply ignores it and keeps answering for
     every tool, so this is backward compatible.
  2. config.approval_enforced, the instance-wide default, for an app that
     provides no partial.

  FAIL-SAFE. Only an explicit `false` from either source turns the gate off. A
  missing partial, one that errors, and any other answer all leave it ON: the
  wrong direction to fail here is to execute something nobody agreed to.

  Read per request, never from the registry cache: the built registry is cached
  for up to five minutes, so a manifest deciding this for itself would keep
  executing ungated for minutes after an operator turned the gate back on.

  @param {object} cfg - the resolved config (helpers/config)
  @param {string} [tool_name] - the tool being called, for an app answering per tool
  @return {boolean} true when a requires_approval tool must wait for a person
{%- endcomment -%}
{% liquid
  assign partial = cfg.approval_partial | default: 'mcp/approval'
  assign answer = null
  try
    function answer = partial, tool_name: tool_name
  catch e
    unless e.message contains "can't find partial"
      log e.message, type: 'mcp.approval_partial_error'
    endunless
    assign answer = null
  endtry

  if answer.enforced == false
    return false
  endif
  if answer.enforced == true
    return true
  endif

  if cfg.approval_enforced == false
    return false
  endif
  return true
%}
